  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nA southwesterly flow aloft will persist across the region over the \nupcoming week. This flow aloft is initially cyclonic in nature, \nwhich will aid in lots of showers and thunderstorms, even during the \novernight, through tonight. Precipitable water values are still \nsomewhat elevated, which will allow for a few stronger thunderstorms \nto produce heavy rainfall rates, at least briefly. Should one of \nthese stronger thunderstorms move over an urban area or a burn scar, \nthere would be flash flooding concerns. \n\nHeading into the weekend the southwesterly flow becomes more \nanticyclonic in nature, with rising heights aloft. Typically this \nwould suppress convection somewhat, but given the forecast lingering \nmoisture near the surface across Southwest and Central Montana and \nrising temperatures, another round of showers and thunderstorms \nlooks to develop Saturday afternoon and evening. Precipitable water \nlooks to decrease at least somewhat, which would favor a lower \nprobability for excessive rainfall. \n\nThe same story plays out Sunday, though with another day of drying, \noverall coverage of showers and thunderstorms becomes more \nquestionable. Continued southwest flow aloft with a few embedded \nwaves will foster additional round of scattered showers and \nthunderstorms into early next week as temperatures trend to near \naverage. Confidence in specifics wanes after Tuesday, though the \nmain takeaway continues to be for additional scattered showers and \nthunderstorms. -AM\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nFlooding risk today: \n\nPrecipitable water values fall ever so slightly today, but still \nlook to be high enough to result in some heavier rainfall rates. \nStorm motions look to be slightly faster, which also would favor \nlesser of a risk for excessive rainfall leading to flash flooding. \nGiven the threshold for flash flooding is so low over the active \nfires in the Pioneers, and that they have already received a wetting \nrain over the previous 24 hours, another flash flood watch was \nissued from 1 pm to 9 pm for today. A few high resolution members \ntake a shower or thunderstorm through this area early this morning, \nbut the probability of this occurring is too low to warrant having \nthe watch begin early this morning. Trends will continue to be \nmonitored for changes in confidence in this area this morning. \n\nAs of now the concern level at Horse Gulch Burn Scar is slightly \nlower. Should this concern level rise even slightly, an additional \nflash flood watch would become necessary. -AM",
